integrated_rules:
For any proposed Election Day national-holiday policy, evaluate two condition sets and map to outcomes as follows.

(A) Supports adopting → Conclude “Election Day should be a national holiday” if the evidence shows all of:
1) The holiday would substantially reduce schedule-related barriers tied to school and work demands for voting;
2) That reduction would increase participation in ways that strengthen democracy (and the policy makes voting feel like a civic celebration rather than a chore);
3) The proposal has broad domestic support (e.g., reflected in support levels like 71% of Democrats and 59% of Republicans) and aligns with international practice (e.g., many OECD countries holding elections on weekends/holidays such as 27 of 36).

(B) Supports rejecting → Conclude “Election Day should not be a national holiday” if the evidence shows all of:
1) The policy is more likely to disadvantage the very groups with the biggest voting barriers—specifically low-income workers, in part because federal law does not require private employers to grant paid holidays and retail/service workers would likely still work;
2) The holiday is less reliable than available alternatives, such as state laws requiring employer time-off for voting or implementing automatic voter registration;
3) Research indicates that having an election holiday by itself is not an effective voter-turnout strategy compared to other voting-access reforms.

Decision when both (A) and (B) are partly/fully supported: apply the same evidential threshold embedded in (A) and (B) (“substantially reduce” and “more likely to disadvantage” plus “less reliable”); adopt only if (A) is supported at that level without (B) being supported at that level, and reject if (B) is supported at that level.
